  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Portability</span>
      <h2>Built against the pattern, not against one control</h2>
      <p class="lede">We attach at three points that every control process has. Nothing is hard-wired to ABC, to counterparty validation, or to one exception platform. A second team adopts it by pointing it at their own systems instead of commissioning a second build.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Generic stage</th><th>Here, today</th><th>What the layer needs from it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Capture</td><td>Source systems, DSL</td><td>Nothing. The amendment a person makes lands here.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Enrich &amp; reconcile</td><td>FODS creating the UC trade, enriched from ISD and book static data</td><td><b>Attachment point.</b> The record and its timestamp at creation, and the static data as it stood at that moment.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Golden store</td><td>Vault</td><td>History for training and for comparable cases.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rule validation</td><td>BRV controls — ABC and counterparty validation in scope</td><td><b>Attachment point.</b> The rule definitions and what each one tests.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Alerting</td><td>Intraday and EOD runs</td><td>Alert records with timestamps.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Exception management</td><td>Snap Explorer, EPE-XACT</td><td><b>Attachment point.</b> Exceptions and their dispositions.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px;"><b>Worth saying in the room.</b> Exceptions already sit in more than one platform — Snap Explorer and EPE-XACT — and one upstream cause can raise items in both on the same day. Nobody sees that today, because no single view spans them. Reading both and grouping across them is useful immediately, and it is also what makes adding a third platform later a small job rather than a new one.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Operating model</span>
      <h2>One day, end to end</h2>
      <p class="lede">The same ten steps, answering four different questions at once. What is touched and how it reverses. What ships first and how it is measured. Whose day changes. What happens when it breaks. The answers are in the table instead of in a meeting.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table class="steps">
        <caption style="caption-side:bottom;text-align:left;padding:10px 14px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;color:var(--soft);">◆ marks a new step. Everything else is today's process, untouched.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>Where it runs</th><th>Who acts</th><th>If it fails</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">1</td><td>Trade arrives</td><td>Source systems, DSL</td><td>No one</td><td class="num">Unchanged</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">2</td><td>UC trade created and enriched</td><td>FODS</td><td>No one</td><td class="num">Unchanged</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent)">3 ◆</td><td><b>Scoring runs</b> on the new UC records, out of band</td><td>Advisory layer, reading a copy</td><td>No one</td><td>Nothing downstream waits on it. The trade proceeds exactly as today.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent)">4 ◆</td><td><b>Fix queue populated</b> — ranked, with reason codes</td><td>New view, outside the control platforms</td><td>No one yet</td><td>Empty queue. Today's process continues.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent)">5 ◆</td><td><b>Ops works the queue</b>, or does not</td><td>Amendments go through the existing upstream process</td><td>Ops, during existing shift hours</td><td>Ignoring the queue leaves today's outcome intact.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">6</td><td>Trade reaches the store; controls run intraday and at EOD</td><td>Vault, BRV</td><td>No one</td><td class="num">Unchanged</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">7</td><td>Alerts raised, exceptions created</td><td>Snap Explorer, EPE-XACT</td><td>No one</td><td class="num">Unchanged</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent)">8 ◆</td><td><b>Triage annotates</b> — rank, cluster, suggested owner, draft narrative</td><td>Read-only over both platforms</td><td>No one</td><td>Exceptions appear unannotated, as today.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">9</td><td>Analyst investigates and disposes</td><td>The exception platform they already use</td><td>Ops analyst, under their own name</td><td class="num">Unchanged</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num" style="color:var(--accent)">10 ◆</td><td><b>Outcomes feed back</b> overnight; scorecard published weekly</td><td>Advisory layer</td><td>No one</td><td>Model ages until the next successful run.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px;"><b>Read the last column top to bottom.</b> Four of the ten steps are new. Not one of them can stop, delay or change a step that runs today. That is the whole integration risk answer, and it is why switching off a single view undoes everything.</p>

    <h3 style="margin:36px 0 6px;">Answered before you ask</h3>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:18px;">The nine questions this proposal reliably attracts, grouped by who usually asks them.</p>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card"><span class="no">CHANGE</span><h3>Does anything live depend on it?</h3><p>No. The layer reads copies and writes to its own view. No code change to FODS, Vault, BRV or either exception platform in Phases 1 to 3. If it is down or late, the day runs as it does today.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">CHANGE</span><h3>What is the rollback?</h3><p>Switch the view off. There is no data migration to reverse and no state to unwind, because the existing process never stopped running underneath it.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">CHANGE</span><h3>Who signs off what?</h3><p>Control owner for the control, ops lead for the queue, Model Risk for the model, this team for delivery. The split is written down in Phase 1, before any model exists.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">PRODUCT</span><h3>What is the first release?</h3><p>One control, one asset class: a ranked queue and a reason-code panel. Drafted narratives and cross-platform clustering are later increments, not part of the first thing that ships.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">PRODUCT</span><h3>How is value measured?</h3><p>Exceptions prevented before the control run, and median time to clear the ones that still happen. Both are baselined in Phase 1, so the improvement is arguable from numbers rather than asserted.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">PRODUCT</span><h3>What is the next team after this one?</h3><p>Whichever control owner wants it. The layer attaches to three generic stages, so a second adopter points it at their own systems rather than commissioning a second build.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">OPS</span><h3>Does my team get more work?</h3><p>The queue moves work earlier in the day rather than adding to it, and nothing is added to the exception queue itself. Phase 3 measures net analyst time; if it goes up, the gate fails.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">OPS</span><h3>What if a prediction is wrong?</h3><p>A false alarm costs the minutes spent looking at one item. A miss returns you to exactly today's process, because the control fires regardless. Neither case is worse than the status quo.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="no">TECH</span><h3>Batch or real time?</h3><p>Scheduled batch runs. The window between UC creation and the control run is hours, so there is no streaming, no latency SLA, and no component of ours in a critical path.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">The plan</span>
      <h2>Four phases, each with a decision point</h2>
      <p class="lede">Only the first is being requested. Each phase ends with a measured result and an explicit choice to continue or stop, so the programme can be halted at three points before anything is relied on operationally.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Time</th><th>What you get at the end</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><b>1 · Baseline</b></td><td class="num">4 weeks</td>
            <td>Measured facts about our own alerts: volume, how many are real, how many share one cause, how many could have been caught earlier. No model is built.</td>
            <td class="num">Proceed or stop</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>2 · Shadow</b></td><td class="num">8 weeks</td>
            <td>Accuracy proven against real outcomes, with predictions logged before the outcome was known. Nothing visible to anyone working a queue.</td>
            <td class="num">Proceed or stop</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>3 · Advisory</b></td><td class="num">8 weeks</td>
            <td>Predictions visible to analysts on one desk, who may act on them or ignore them. Every alert is still raised in full.</td>
            <td class="num">Proceed or stop</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>4 · Extend</b></td><td class="num">ongoing</td>
            <td>Further controls, asset classes and teams, each proven the same way before it goes live.</td>
            <td class="num">Governance sign-off</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px;"><b>What makes the gates real.</b> The accuracy thresholds for each phase are agreed with the control owner before that phase begins, not argued about afterwards. A phase that misses its number ends the programme rather than extending it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Phase 1 output</span>
      <h2>The baseline we do not have yet</h2>
      <p class="lede">Nobody can approve a benefit case against numbers nobody has measured. Phase 1 exists to fill this table in — separately for ABC and for counterparty validation, since the whole point of running both is to compare them — with the control owner agreeing each figure before anything else is discussed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Measure (per control)</th><th>How we get it</th><th>Today</th><th>Target</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>BRV alerts raised per day</td><td>Alert log, 24 months, by rule and asset class</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num">unchanged</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Share closed as no issue</td><td>Disposition codes on closed exceptions</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">set at gate</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Alerts sharing one root cause</td><td>Clustering of historic alerts by book, cpty, source</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">set at gate</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Split of intraday vs EOD alerts</td><td>Alert type, and UC creation time vs alert raise time</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EOD alerts that were predictable intraday</td><td>Replay: was the cause visible at UC creation?</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">set at gate</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>One cause raising items in both platforms</td><td>Match Snap Explorer and EPE-XACT items on book, cpty, source</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">set at gate</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Median time to clear an exception</td><td>Raise-to-close, by platform and by team</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">set at gate</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Counterparty failures that were a matching issue</td><td>Resolution notes on closed CPTY exceptions: wrong entity level, name variant, incomplete ISD setup</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">set at gate</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Breaches missed by the controls</td><td>Issues found by other means, logged after the fact</td><td class="num"><span class="tbd">to measure</span></td><td class="num">no increase</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px;"><b>Why this table is empty.</b> Putting invented numbers in a management pack is the fastest way to lose the room, and the first question will be where they came from. The honest version — a measurement plan with named owners for each figure — is also the version that survives challenge.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Make it concrete</span>
      <h2>What an analyst actually sees</h2>
      <p class="lede">Abstract talk about models is where these proposals die. Put these two on the screen in the meeting — one per control — and let people challenge the lines. Every line is meant to be challengeable.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mock">
      <div class="bar">
        <span>Pre-cut-off fix queue</span>
        <span><b>Rank 3 of 41</b></span>
        <span>14:22 · before the intraday run</span>
      </div>
      <div class="inner">
        <dl class="kv">
          <dt>UC trade ref</dt><dd>UC-2026-10-0048812</dd>
          <dt>Book</dt><dd>CF-2291 · GDM Cash Bonds</dd>
          <dt>Counterparty</dt><dd>CPTY-004417</dd>
          <dt>Source</dt><dd>DSL → FODS · UC created 14:06 · in Vault 14:11</dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="score">
          <span class="v">0.87</span>
          <span class="lab">predicted to fail&nbsp; ABC — active book check</span>
        </div>

        <div class="reasons">
          <div class="reason"><span class="b">›</span><span>Book CF-2291 moved to <b>inactive-pending</b> in reference data at 09:14 today; 6 trades already booked to it since.</span></div>
          <div class="reason"><span class="b">›</span><span>Same book failed ABC on 11 of the last 30 business days.</span></div>
          <div class="reason"><span class="b">›</span><span>Of 14 comparable cases, 13 were resolved by rebooking to the successor book; 1 was a genuine late trade.</span></div>
        </div>

        <dl class="kv">
          <dt>Suggested action</dt><dd>Confirm book status with the desk and rebook, or this raises an ABC alert intraday and an exception at EOD.</dd>
          <dt>Would clear</dt><dd>This item and 5 others on the same book — one cause, six alerts, across both exception platforms.</dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="decide">
          <span><b>Model</b> ranks and explains</span>
          <span><b>Analyst</b> decides and disposes</span>
          <span><b>BRV</b> fires regardless at EOD</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mock" style="margin-top:18px;">
      <div class="bar">
        <span>Pre-control fix queue</span>
        <span><b>Rank 1 of 41</b></span>
        <span>10:42 · counterparty validation</span>
      </div>
      <div class="inner">
        <dl class="kv">
          <dt>UC trade ref</dt><dd>UC-2026-10-0048790</dd>
          <dt>Cpty as presented</dt><dd>"Meridian Capital Markets (Asia) Pte"</dd>
          <dt>ISD match</dt><dd>no active BERID at the level presented</dd>
          <dt>Source</dt><dd>DSL → FODS · UC created 10:31</dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="score">
          <span class="v">0.93</span>
          <span class="lab">predicted to fail&nbsp; counterparty validation</span>
        </div>

        <div class="reasons">
          <div class="reason"><span class="b">›</span><span>Closest active ISD entity by name, country and sector is the <b>group parent</b>, GRID ID 70-441-882 — the Singapore subsidiary was onboarded 3 days ago and still has no BERID.</span></div>
          <div class="reason"><span class="b">›</span><span>9 of the last 11 trades presented under this name were rebooked to the subsidiary once its setup completed.</span></div>
          <div class="reason"><span class="b">›</span><span>Confidence in the suggested entity match: <b>high</b>. Confidence that the record is incomplete rather than wrong: <b>high</b>.</span></div>
