    <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px;"><b>Read the last line of each card aloud in the meeting.</b> The control still runs whatever the analyst does, and still decides. The model only bought the hours to fix the cause first. Note also the difference between the two cards: the first is a fact that a watch could have spotted, the second is a judgement about which legal entity was meant. That difference is the whole argument for running both controls in the pilot.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Prerequisites</span>
      <h2>What has to be true before any of this works</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ask" style="border-color:var(--crit);margin-bottom:20px;">
      <span class="tag" style="color:var(--crit)">The two that can kill it</span>
      <p><b>We need to know which past alerts were real.</b> The models learn from how exceptions were closed. If almost everything is closed under one generic code, there is nothing to learn from. Then the honest answer is that this cannot be built yet, and the first fix is better closure codes, not a model.</p>
      <p><b>We need a key that joins the records.</b> To show that one cause raised items in both Snap Explorer and EPE-XACT, we need an identifier that follows a trade through FODS, Vault, the alert and both exception records. Without one, grouping across platforms is out of scope.</p>
      <p>Both are checked in the first two weeks of Phase 1, before anything is committed. Finding out they fail is a cheap and useful result.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Data</h3>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li>24 months of alerts and exceptions for both controls, with disposition codes and free-text resolution notes</li>
          <li>UC trade records with creation timestamps from FODS</li>
          <li>ISD and book static data as a change history, point in time — not just the current state</li>
          <li>Rule definitions: what each control tests and against which fields</li>
          <li>For counterparty matching: entity names, aliases and group hierarchy, not identifiers alone</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Point-in-time correctness</h3>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li>Features must reflect what was knowable at UC creation, not what is known now</li>
          <li>Reference data read as at the trade timestamp</li>
          <li>Without this the shadow results will look excellent and mean nothing</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Environment &amp; access</h3>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li>Read entitlements to both exception platforms, FODS and Vault</li>
          <li>A compute environment with an approved Python package mirror</li>
          <li>Somewhere durable to write the prediction log</li>
          <li>Internal LLM gateway access, only from Phase 3</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Governance &amp; people</h3>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li>Model inventory entry and risk tiering, opened at Phase 2</li>
          <li>Data access approvals and a named information owner</li>
          <li>Around two hours a week from a control SME</li>
          <li>An exception analyst to validate a sample of labels</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Approach</span>
      <h2>How this gets a yes</h2>
      <p class="lede">Proposals like this rarely fail on the idea. They fail because the governance conversation starts after the pitch instead of inside it. So we answer those questions first, and the review becomes a confirmation rather than a discovery.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stack">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="no">01</span>
        <h3>Bring the risk classification already done</h3>
        <p>The question has shifted from "is this a model?" to "what is the risk?", and many firms now register analytical tools as models by default. So we will not spend the meeting on that boundary. We arrive with the assessment filled in and accept whichever tier the assessor applies.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="no">02</span>
        <h3>Never score a person</h3>
        <p>Everything is scored at book, counterparty, instrument or source-system level. Nothing is scored by trader or by individual. Profiling people is the one choice that pushes an internal tool into the most heavily regulated bracket, and nothing here needs it. We rule it out now and say so, because a limit we volunteer reads very differently from one imposed on us.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="no">03</span>
        <h3>Use the words the assessment will use</h3>
        <p>An internal AI system is judged on four tests: does it do a narrow procedural task, does it improve work a person already does, does it spot patterns without displacing a human judgement, and is it preparatory rather than the decision itself. All four describe this. We write the scope in those words from the first draft, so the classification argues itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="no">04</span>
        <h3>Commit to the four evidence activities</h3>
        <p>Test before use, monitor during use, independent review by someone who is neither the owner nor a user, and remediate what is found. The substance of a review is documented evidence that these four actually happened. Offering them before being asked removes most of the negotiation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="no">05</span>
        <h3>Make Model Risk and Compliance co-authors</h3>
        <p>Show them the workflow while the design can still change, and ask what would worry them. Supervisors have said plainly that engaging early on a pilot is welcome, and that a pilot which uncovers a gap is not itself a criticism — which is exactly what the shadow phase is. An assessor who helped shape the design does not need to be convinced of it later.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="no">06</span>
        <h3>Ask for the smallest reversible thing</h3>
        <p>The request on the table is four weeks of analysis on data already held. Not a programme, not a platform, not a budget line. Every later phase is a separate decision made with evidence that does not exist yet.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin:32px 0 12px;">The assessment, pre-filled</h3>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Our proposed answer</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Model or tool</td><td>Register as a model or critical tool from Phase 2</td><td>We are not going to argue the boundary; govern it at the stricter reading</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Impact if wrong</td><td class="num">Low</td><td>No control decision depends on the output; the worst case of a wrong prediction is today's process</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pervasiveness</td><td class="num">Low</td><td>Two controls, one asset class, one desk, one ops team</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Explainability</td><td class="num">High</td><td>Tree ensembles with reason codes, no third-party model, no language model anywhere in scoring</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Autonomy</td><td class="num">None</td><td>A person disposes of every item; nothing closes itself</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Profiling of individuals</td><td class="num">None</td><td>Features are book, counterparty, instrument and source only — never trader-level</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Data leaving the bank</td><td class="num">None</td><td>Internal sources, internal compute, no external service</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reversibility</td><td class="num">Full</td><td>Switching off a view restores the current process exactly</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Controls</span>
      <h2>Governance, in the bank's own language</h2>
      <p class="lede">Half the resistance to an AI proposal is the suspicion that it has not been thought through as a controlled change. Arriving with this already mapped removes the objection before it is raised.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>How this proposal answers it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Model inventory &amp; tiering</td><td>Registered at Phase 2 as a non-control, advisory model, with a named accountable owner from the first day of Phase 1.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scope of what is scored</td><td>Books, counterparties, instruments and source systems. No individual is scored, profiled or ranked, in this or any later phase.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Independent review</td><td>Validation by someone who is neither the model owner nor a user, with documented evidence of testing before use and monitoring during use.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Explainability</td><td>SHAP reason codes on every prediction, traceable to source fields. Tree ensembles only; no deep learning, and no language model anywhere in the scoring path.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reproducibility</td><td>Model version, feature set, training window and random seed logged per prediction in MLflow, so any score can be reproduced on demand.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Challenger / benchmark</td><td>The current rules-only process is the benchmark. The model must beat it on the agreed measures or the gate fails.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Performance monitoring</td><td>Weekly scorecard from Phase 2 onward; drift alerting on inputs and on accuracy, with a named owner.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Human in the loop</td><td>Mandatory at every stage. No automated disposition is in scope.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Audit trail</td><td>Every prediction, reason code, model version and analyst action logged and reproducible, retained to policy.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Data lineage &amp; residency</td><td>Existing internal sources only, inside existing permissions. No new data, no client data leaving the bank, no external model service.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Change control</td><td>Model versions released through the same change process as the dashboards this team already runs.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Kill switch</td><td>Turning the layer off restores today's process exactly, because today's process never stopped running underneath it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Preparation</span>
      <h2>What they will ask, and the answer</h2>
      <p class="lede">Rehearse these. The questions are predictable, and answering them crisply in the room is most of what decides the outcome.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="obj">
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">What if the model misses a real breach?</div>
        <div class="ans">Then the rule catches it at EOD, exactly as it does today. <strong>The worst case of a wrong prediction is the current process.</strong> The model can only add an earlier warning; it cannot take a later one away.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">This is a black box. How do I explain it to the regulator?</div>
        <div class="ans">You explain the rules, because the rules are still the control. The model is a workflow aid outside the control boundary, registered and monitored, and every output it produces names the fields behind it.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">I do not want AI making control decisions.</div>
        <div class="ans">Neither do we. <strong>It makes none.</strong> It reorders a work queue and writes an explanation. Every disposition is an analyst's, logged under their name, as it is today.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">Our data quality will not support this.</div>
        <div class="ans">Possibly. Phase 2 answers that in eight weeks using data we already hold, with nothing in production and no spend committed. A shadow run that fails is a cheap, useful answer.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">We have seen AI pilots that went nowhere.</div>
        <div class="ans">So the exit criteria are agreed before each stage starts, not argued afterwards. <strong>We have named the conditions under which we stop</strong>, including at Phase 1.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">Who is accountable when it is wrong?</div>
        <div class="ans">The control owner owns the control and the decision, unchanged. This team owns model performance and reports it weekly. The split is written down at Phase 1, before any model exists.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">What does it cost?</div>
        <div class="ans">Phases 1 and 2 use existing data, existing infrastructure and this team's current capacity, plus roughly two hours a week from a control SME. No new spend is requested until the Phase 2 gate.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">Why two controls rather than one?</div>
        <div class="ans">They test different propositions. ABC is deterministic, so it tells us whether a reference-data watch is sufficient. Counterparty validation turns on which entity was actually meant, so it tells us whether a model adds anything a rule cannot. <strong>One control tells you whether that control improved; two tell you what the rest of the estate should do.</strong></div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">Will this be treated as a model?</div>
        <div class="ans">We have assumed yes and proposed to be governed as one from Phase 2, rather than argue the boundary. The assessment is pre-filled in this pack — impact, pervasiveness, explainability, autonomy, profiling — and we will accept whichever tier the assessor applies.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="q">Why not just tune the rules instead?</div>
        <div class="ans">We should, and Phase 1 will show where. Rule mining and threshold calibration come out of the same baseline. <strong>The two are complementary</strong>: better rules reduce the volume, prediction moves what remains earlier.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sechead">
      <span class="kicker">Scope &amp; needs</span>
      <h2>Keep the pilot deliberately small</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Pilot scope</h3>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li><b>ABC — active book check</b>: the deterministic case, testing whether a reference-data watch is enough</li>
          <li><b>Counterparty validation</b> against ISD: the matching case, testing whether a model earns its place</li>
          <li>One asset class and one region</li>
          <li>One exception platform end to end, with the second read-only for cross-platform clustering</li>
          <li>24 months of historic alerts with dispositions, for both controls</li>
          <li>Built against the generic stages — every other control out of scope until a gate is passed</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>What we need to start</h3>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li>Read access to exception history and disposition codes from Snap Explorer and EPE-XACT</li>
          <li>BRV alert records for both controls, with rule, timestamp and run type (intraday or EOD)</li>
          <li>UC trade records from FODS with their creation timestamps</li>
          <li>ISD counterparty records and their change history, including GRID ID and BERID, point in time</li>
          <li>Book status change history</li>
          <li>Around two hours a week from a control SME for each control</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p class="note"><b>About the figures in this pack.</b> Every number on the sample cards is made up as an illustration, and every cell in the baseline table is deliberately empty. Producing the real ones is what Phase 1 is for. Presenting estimates as measurements is the fastest way to lose a control audience.</p>
  </footer>

